    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Tests for {@link Soundex#US_ENGLISH_SIMPLIFIED}, which implements the Simplified Soundex algorithm. <p>The key difference from the standard {@link Soundex#US_ENGLISH} variant is how 'H' and 'W' are treated: in Simplified Soundex they act as <em>separators</em> (behaving like the vowels A, E, I, O, U, Y), whereas in standard Soundex they are completely silent (ignored entirely). A separator resets the \"last digit\" tracker, so two identical consonant codes on either side of an H or W are <strong>not</strong> merged — each produces its own digit.</p> @see <a href=\"http://west-penwith.org.uk/misc/soundex.htm\">Simplified Soundex reference</a>"
   },
   {
